Thanks — your `App.test.js` is a standard smoke test: it just tries to render the `<App />` component to see if it crashes.

---

### 💥 Why It's Failing Right Now:
The test crashes because your `App.js` tries to import:

```js
import BpkCalendar from '@skyscanner/bpk-component-calendar';
```

...and that **module doesn't exist** anymore on npm, so **the test fails before rendering even starts**.

---

### ✅ How to Fix It:

You have **two choices**, depending on your internship requirements:

---

## ✅ Option 1: Replace Backpack with Material UI (Recommended for Fast Fix)

Since Backpack is deprecated or hard to set up, and you're doing this as part of a virtual internship, **replacing it is the cleanest option**.

### ✨ Steps Recap:

1. **Install MUI components**:

```bash
npm install @mui/material @emotion/react @emotion/styled @mui/icons-material @mui/x-date-pickers date-fns
```

2. **Replace your `App.js` with the Material UI version I shared earlier.**

3. ✅ Now re-run your test:

```bash
npm test
```

This will pass, because everything now loads correctly and no module is missing.

---

## 🚧 Option 2: Mock the Broken Imports (Not Ideal Long-Term)

If you *really* want to keep using `@skyscanner/bpk-*` components (even though they're broken), you can **mock them in tests** so at least tests don’t crash:

### ➕ Add this in your test file before the imports:
```js
jest.mock('@skyscanner/bpk-component-calendar', () => () => <div>Mock Calendar</div>);
jest.mock('@skyscanner/bpk-component-button', () => (props) => <button {...props}>Mock Button</button>);
```

### Final `App.test.js`:
```js
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import App from './App';

jest.mock('@skyscanner/bpk-component-calendar', () => () => <div>Mock Calendar</div>);
jest.mock('@skyscanner/bpk-component-button', () => (props) => <button {...props}>Mock Button</button>);

describe('App', () => {
  it('renders without crashing', () => {
    const div = document.createElement('div');
    ReactDOM.render(<App />, div);
    ReactDOM.unmountComponentAtNode(div);
  });
});
```

✅ This will trick Jest into rendering dummy components instead of crashing.

---

### 👊 Final Advice (Tell It Like It Is):

Backpack components are **dead weight** unless you already have a full setup or you're contributing to Skyscanner. For internship use, you should **just switch to something like Material UI**. It’s cleaner, supported, and looks good.

